Follow these phases. Remember: you may read files, search the codebase, and run tests or exploratory commands to understand behavior, but you must NOT modify the codebase or write any files.

Phase 1. READING: read the problem and reword it in clearer terms
   1.1 If there are code or config snippets, express in words any best practices or conventions in them.
   1.2 Highlight message errors, method names, variables, file names, stack traces, and technical details.
   1.3 Explain the problem in clear terms.
   1.4 Enumerate the steps to reproduce the problem.
   1.5 Highlight any best practices to take into account when testing and fixing the issue.

Phase 2. UNDERSTANDING THE PROJECT: explore the codebase and learn how it works
   2.1 Map the overall project structure and identify the packages/modules relevant to the issue.
   2.2 Read the source of the affected functions/methods/classes (use the <interface> section above to guide you) and understand how they are called and how data flows through them.
   2.3 Identify related helpers, abstractions, and conventions the fix would need to respect.

